# Line endings are normalised in the repository and checked out as LF on every
# platform, including Windows.
#
# This is not tidiness. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by
# default, so without this file a Windows clone rewrites build.sh with CRLF
# endings, bash reads the shebang as `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the build dies
# looking for an interpreter named "bash\r" -- before the compiler is ever
# reached, with an error that names neither the file nor the cause.
#
# The shader matters for a second reason: crt.fs is #load'ed into the binary as
# a string literal, so its bytes are part of the program rather than an input to
# it. A CRLF checkout would change what the compiler embeds.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.odin  text eol=lf
*.fs    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
